<Project>

  <!--
    THIS FILE DELIBERATELY DOES NOT IMPORT THE ROOT Directory.Build.props, for the
    same reason src/Ft8Sharp/Directory.Build.props does not (HM-DEC-152).

    MSBuild walks up from a project looking for the first Directory.Build.props and
    stops at it. The usual courtesy is to import the one above before adding to it.
    Not importing it is the whole point here, and the absence of that import is a
    decision rather than an oversight.

    The root file injects Version, and with it AssemblyVersion,
    AssemblyInformationalVersion and BuildStampUtc, into every project in the
    solution. Ft8Sharp.Deep is a library with its own licence, its own NOTICE and
    its own reason to exist, and a library that announces itself as version 1.12.48
    of a desktop program is wrong on the first line a reader of the package sees.
    HM-DEC-063 exists so the tree has one answer to "what version is this app";
    Ft8Sharp.Deep is not the app.

    0.1.0 BECAUSE IT DELEGATES AND NOTHING ELSE.

    Unit 245 creates this library to satisfy step 1 of PHASE_PLAN.md, whose whole
    point is that BEHAVIOUR DOES NOT CHANGE. Ft8DeepSlotDecoder holds an
    Ft8SlotDecoder built with the parameters it was handed and returns that
    decoder's Ft8SlotResult unchanged - every count and every message, in order.
    There is no ordered statistics decoding here, no subtraction, no re-sync, no
    per-message SNR and no cross-slot combining. Those are steps 2 to 6 and not one
    line of any of them is in this version.

    So the scoreboard reads two columns tonight and they agree decode for decode,
    which is a statement about the SEAM and about the harness wiring and about
    nothing else. From the unit that takes step 2, a difference between the columns
    is attributable to exactly one named change, and that is what this version buys.

    A 0.x rather than a 1.x, and the reason is the same as Ft8Sharp's own first
    version: a 1.x would claim a maturity this library has not got. The minor is
    bumped when it gains a capability of its own.

    0.2.0 at unit 246, which is that bump: this library now runs the port's
    per-candidate loop itself and carries an ordered statistics decoder, so it can
    reach codewords the port cannot. What it may NOT do is decide that any of them
    is a message - the port's parity gate and CRC-14 gate remain the only
    acceptance, and there is still no checksum in this library.

    0.3.0 at unit 247, which is the next such bump: this library gained the soft
    combiner, the pairing rule and the repeat decoder that carry one station's
    ratios across slots, so it can reach codewords no single slot holds.

    0.4.0 at unit 248, and it is a bump for the same reason again. This library
    can now read a candidate BELOW the waterfall's grid: it mixes the slot's
    samples to complex baseband about a candidate's eight tones, low-pass
    filters and decimates them, searches sub-symbol time and sub-hertz frequency
    by Costas correlation, and extracts the soft values at whatever position it
    finds. Every figure this phase has recorded before tonight was measured at
    the one placement where the coarse grid has nothing to lose - exactly on a
    bin centre and exactly on a sub-block boundary - and unit 248's task 1
    measured what the rest of the cell costs.

    What it still may NOT do is decide that anything is a message. The port's
    parity gate and CRC-14 gate remain the only acceptance and there is still no
    checksum in this library, at most one extra submission per candidate.

    0.5.0 at unit 251, and it is a bump for the same reason again, but for a
    capability of a different kind: this library can now say, in decibels in the
    2500 Hz reference bandwidth the published FT8 figures are quoted against,
    HOW STRONG ONE DECODED MESSAGE WAS. Ft8DeepSignalToNoise reads the power in
    the tone that was transmitted against the seven tones that were not, at the
    same instant, over the symbols whose windows lie inside the slot, and
    carries the per-bin ratio to the reference by 10 log10(2500 / 6.25) =
    26.0206 dB - a derivation from the tone spacing and the symbol period in
    which the decimation, the filter and the sample rate all cancel.
    Ft8DeepMessageSymbols recovers the symbol sequence it needs by packing the
    decoded message again, with a round trip through the message layer as the
    guard, because the decode result hands back text and carries no bits.

    THIS IS THE FIRST TYPE IN THE TREE THAT IS ACTUALLY A SIGNAL-TO-NOISE RATIO,
    and it says which definition and in which bandwidth in its own remarks. It
    is PURE AND REPORT-ONLY: it touches no ratio, no gate, no count and no
    decision, and Ft8DeepSlotDecoder does not call it.

    What it still may NOT do is decide that anything is a message, and this
    version does not move that line one inch.

    0.6.0 at unit 252, and it is a bump for the same reason again: the ordered
    statistics search gained a SECOND KNOB. Ft8DeepOsdSettings.Window says how
    many of the least reliable basis positions the order's flips may fall in,
    and Ft8DeepOrderedStatistics.Search starts its enumeration there instead of
    at 0. That is the whole change - one integer and one loop bound - and it
    costs 1 + sum over i of C(window, i) re-encodings a candidate instead of
    1 + sum over i of C(91, i), which is what makes an order the whole basis
    could not afford affordable over part of it. It is Fossorier and Lin's own
    segmentation of the most reliable basis, from the 1995 paper's published
    description and nowhere else, cited at the point of use.

    THE DEFAULT DID NOT MOVE IN THIS VERSION and the full-basis path is
    bit-for-bit what 0.5.0 did: same re-encoding count, same recovered codeword,
    asserted by
    Ft8DeepOrderedStatisticsTests.TheCostOfAnOrderInAWindowIsTheNumberOfSubsetsOfTheWindow
    rather than assumed, because every figure in docs/unit246-osd.md is a
    measurement of that path.

    What it still may NOT do is decide that anything is a message. A window is
    not a threshold, not a confidence and not an acceptance rule; the stage still
    produces exactly one codeword per candidate offered and submits exactly that
    one to the port's gates, and docs/unit252-osd-window.md section 4 writes the
    false-accept arithmetic out either side of the change.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <Version>0.6.0</Version>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    AND NO GIT COMMIT EITHER, for the reason recorded beside Ft8Sharp's own copy of
    this property: the SDK appends the repository's source revision to
    AssemblyInformationalVersion by itself, without help from the root file, so
    dropping the inheritance above is not enough on its own.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <IncludeSourceRevisionInInformationalVersion>false</IncludeSourceRevisionInInformationalVersion>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
